From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Subject: amdv: cap the LLDV VSVDB peak to the content's own mastering peak
 
In low-latency (player-led) Dolby Vision output the DV core takes its target
peak from the SINK'S VSVDB, not from the set_target_max_lum argument. That is
why the same title is graded correctly through VS10 HDR10 output - which uses
amdv_target_max[][] - and wrong in LLDV.
 
Consequence: a display advertising (or being told, via our vsvdb.maxlum
override) a 3000-nit peak, playing content mastered at ~100 nits, leaves the
DM with ~30x headroom. It then has essentially nothing to map, and every L2
trim scales to nothing - reported on-box as a "Positive Lift Offset Trim" test
pattern showing no lift at all in player-led DV while being correct in HDR10
output. Confirmed by dropping the injected peak to 100: the lift reappeared.
 
Fix, ported from avdvplus R9 (amdolby_vision.c:5668, its LL-only VSVDB block):
when low latency is engaged and the source is Dolby Vision, clamp the v2 VSVDB
maximum-luminance field handed to the control path down to the stream's own
source_max_pq (the ETSI base block at md_buf[66..67]). Never raises it, so a
title graded above the panel is unaffected.
 
This edits only new_m_dovi_setting.vsvdb_tbl - the in-memory copy passed to
multi_control_path. It deliberately does NOT touch force_vsvdb/vsvdb_data:
that path re-latches the EDID, which blanks the sink for seconds mid-playback
(the reason Kodi's aml_dv_apply_vsvdb carries a dv_cap liveness guard), and a
cap that tracks content cannot live there.
 
avdvplus also forces the VSVDB minimum luminance to 0 in this block. That is
not ported: the panels checked here already advertise min = 0, so it would be
an untestable no-op, and lifting a non-zero sink black is a separate decision.
 
Only v2 VSVDBs are handled (version field == 2), matching the only format our
Kodi-side injector understands; v0/v1 blocks are left alone.
 
--- a/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
@@ -11158,4 +11158,44 @@
 	new_m_dovi_setting.input[IPCORE2_ID].valid = 1;
 
+	/* LLDV: the DM takes its target peak from the sink's VSVDB, so cap it to
+	 * the content's own mastering peak - otherwise low-peak titles get so
+	 * much headroom that the trims map to nothing. Ported from avdvplus R9.
+	 * In-memory table only; never the EDID (that re-latch blanks the sink).
+	 */
+	if (dolby_vision_ll_policy >= DOLBY_VISION_LL_YUV422 &&
+	    new_m_dovi_setting.input[0].src_format == FORMAT_DOVI &&
+	    new_m_dovi_setting.input[0].in_md &&
+	    new_m_dovi_setting.input[0].in_md_size > 67 &&
+	    new_m_dovi_setting.vsvdb_len >= 8) {
+		unsigned char *x = &new_m_dovi_setting.vsvdb_tbl[5];
+		const unsigned char *md =
+			(const unsigned char *)new_m_dovi_setting.input[0].in_md;
+
+		/* v2 only: max_pq = 2055 + 65 * field (bits 7:3) */
+		if (((x[0] >> 5) & 0x07) == 2) {
+			u16 vsvdb_max = 2055 + 65 * (x[2] >> 3);
+			u16 src_max_pq = ((u16)md[66] << 8) | md[67];
+
+			if (src_max_pq && src_max_pq < vsvdb_max) {
+				u16 idx = 0;
+
+				/* round up so the cap never lands below the
+				 * content's own peak
+				 */
+				if (src_max_pq > 2055)
+					idx = (src_max_pq - 2055 + 64) / 65;
+				if (idx > 31)
+					idx = 31;
+				if ((x[2] >> 3) != idx) {
+					x[2] = (x[2] & 0x07) |
+					       ((idx & 0x1F) << 3);
+					if (debug_dolby & 1)
+						pr_dv_dbg("LLDV: vsvdb peak capped to source %u pq (idx %u, was %u pq)\n",
+							  src_max_pq, idx, vsvdb_max);
+				}
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	if (debug_dolby & 0x400)
 		do_gettimeofday(&start);
